<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pagasetic Gulf</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gulf of Volos, Gulf of Pagasae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pagasetic Gulf, or Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volos">Volos</a>, is a nearly enclosed inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, sheltered by the Pelion peninsula. The ancient cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagasae">Pagasae</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrias">Demetrias</a> stood on its shores, and the modern port of Volos lies at its head. From 1832 to 1881 it marked part of the northern border of the Kingdom of Greece.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pagasetic-gulf.jpg" alt="Pagasetic Gulf" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Flashdart2 at Greek Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pagasetic-gulf-east-side.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
